ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: green
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
- The 14.9% post-first-value "residual reuse" is fully explained by coincidental same-length value sampling — confirmed by the fixed-length control collapsing RoPE tax to exactly 0.0 and c/b to 1.0. No emergent position-stability; pure combinatorics of value-length collisions.
- The 5–6% "structural win over APC" (c−a) is mostly the small fraction of seeds/fields where the FIRST variable value happens to match length, slightly extending the stable prefix — i.e. still a prefix effect, not genuine post-shift reuse.
- The 42–44% token-level ceiling is just (#schema tokens)/(#total tokens) for the chosen schema/value ratio — a definitional upper bound, not evidence of a reusable mechanism.
- APC's 6–13% capture is the expected E[prefix length] until first value token under random value sampling — basic order-statistics, not a caching pathology.
CONFOUNDS:
- Tokenizer is a deterministic word/punct splitter (no BPE); real BPE could either merge across schema/value boundaries (lowering ceiling) or split values into more tokens (raising it) — direction unknown but the RoPE-shift mechanism is invariant to this, so it does not rescue the claim.
- RoPE modeled as exact-absolute-position equality is a first-order proxy: in practice attention is somewhat tolerant to small position shifts (cos/sin smoothness), so 14.9% may UNDER-state usable reuse — but recovering it requires tolerance/re-anchor = CacheBlend, which the packet already concedes.
- Schema/value ratio and value-length distribution are author-chosen; they set both the ceiling and the shift rate. Results are reported across N∈{4,8,16}, cx∈{3,6} and are stable, which mitigates this.
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
- "Bytewise/allclose KV equality at shifted positions" is asserted false via the position-equality proxy; not verified against real KV tensors (acknowledged as L1).
- Independence/stationarity of value lengths across calls — real agents may have bursty same-length results (pagination, fixed enums) that would inflate (c) without changing the mechanism.
- Tool-mix uniformity: cache-first-call-of-each-tool assumption ignores eviction; under realistic cache pressure, APC's 6–13% would drop further, but so would (c) — relative gap (5–6%) is the load-bearing number and is unlikely to widen materially.
STATISTICAL_GAPS:
- 8 seeds is thin but the effect sizes and direction are highly consistent across the 6 (N,cx) cells and the fixed-vs-var contrast is essentially deterministic (tax = 0.000 in control) — no variance/CI reported, but the sign and ~5–6% magnitude are robust enough for a KILL.
- No formal test that c−a < 10% (pre-reg threshold); given max observed c−a = 0.063 across all cells, a one-sided test is trivially satisfied — would still be nice to report SEs.
- No sensitivity sweep on value-length distribution (e.g. heavy-tail, bimodal) — could matter for absolute (c) but mechanism (RoPE shift on first variable-length value) is invariant.
RATIONALE: The "surprising" residual is exactly predicted by basic theory: RoPE makes KV a function of absolute position, so a single variable-LENGTH value span shifts all 85% downstream schema tokens off their cached positions; what survives (14.9%) is exactly coincidental-length alignment, proven by the fixed-length control hitting tax=0.0 and c/b=1.0. The claimed mechanism ("position-stable WITHOUT re-anchoring") is not just unsupported — it is the negation of what the diag_where.csv breakdown (3971/3971 pre-value vs 3448/23199 post-value) directly measures. Prior-art pincer is tight and cited: Prompt Cache (Gim et al.) realizes the position-independent ceiling, CacheBlend is the R(Δ) re-anchor — leaving the claim sandwiched into either (a) a 5–6% sub-threshold weaker special case of Prompt Cache, or (b) CacheBlend itself. Same RoPE wall that killed EXP-0009 (0.35–1.46%) fires here at 5–6% — more legible structure, same physics. Honest negative, correctly designed-around the Jensen-floor trap, correctly confronted the RoPE trap, correctly died on it. GREEN on the kill.
